WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.800
But, hang on,
aren't we part of the Earth?

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.520
We're organic creatures,

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.880
made of the same molecules
as everything else on our planet -

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.480
one interconnected ecosystem
that keeps us all alive.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.680
But are we superior?
Different? Or special?

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:34.680
Why do we so often feel, well...
separate from the Earth?

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.240
In fact, not everyone does.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.160
Throughout human history
lots of cultures have viewed

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.520
their relationship to
the Earth in other ways.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.640
Many still do.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.480
Animism - the belief that
spirit infuses objects,

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.480
nature and the whole of existence -

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.200
is a fundamental principle
of many indigenous societies.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.520
In Taoism, cosmic energy -
or life force - is everywhere

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.400
and is constantly seeking harmony
and balance between all things.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.360
Ancient Andean civilisations
placed the spirit of Pachamama,

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.760
or Mother Earth, at the very centre
of their worldview.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.920
And, today, for many indigenous
people of the world,

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.160
like the U'wa tribe in Colombia,

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.720
the Earth is not just the dust
from which human beings are made,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.520
it's the spirit of their people
and ancestors.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:26.840
It's their history.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.600
It is life itself.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.360
This isn't always an easy idea

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.320
for people in today's
Western societies to grasp.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.360
The Druids of Ancient Britain
did grasp it -

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.200
focusing their spiritual practices

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.560
on a reverence
and connection with nature.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.240
But when Christianity
spread through Europe,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.680
animism was condemned
as pagan and savage.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.960
European colonialism enforced
these views around the globe,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.360
in places like
the Americas and Australia -

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.560
crushing the indigenous peoples
and their nature wisdom.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.160
Since the birth of agriculture,

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.520
humans had been manipulating
the world around them,

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.720
harnessing, dividing
and selling its natural resources.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.440
But under colonization,

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.560
the Earth became a place to be
conquered, dominated,

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.320
farmed, fished, plundered
and mined, on a vast scale.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.560
Was there any scientific thinking
behind our ever-increasing

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.840
control over the natural world?

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.560
17th Century Europe thought so. 

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.080
In fact thought was
all the rage then,

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.160
as French philosopher
Rene Descartes,

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.160
and other others,
like Sir Isaac Newton,

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.680
helped to kick off
the Enlightenment -

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.160
an era that prized reasoning,
science and order.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.720
A new view of the world emerged -

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.480
with human beings as
the thinking, rational, masters,

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.760
and nature as our unthinking,
unfeeling servant.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.760
Standing apart from her,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.200
humans investigated nature's
ultimate questions,

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.320
dissecting her into smaller
and smaller pieces.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.280
This brought us great power.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.760
And with great power, comes...

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.680
... you know the rest.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.680
The Anthropocene Age had begun -

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.800
an epoch defined by
one dominant species.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:18.880
Us.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:24.440
On a planet that is billions
of years old, in just 500 years,

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.880
much of the human race
has come to see itself

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.240
as the rulers
and center of all life.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:34.120
Today, we are still trying to bend
the planet's environment,

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.280
processes and biodiversity
to our will.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.160
Our vast, sprawling cities

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.640
have pushed nature
out of sight and out of mind.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.120
The world is attempting to meet the
urgent challenge of climate change.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.480
But can anything fundamentally change

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.520
while we still see ourselves
as somehow separate from Earth?

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.200
There are signs of hope. Other voices
are starting to be heard once again.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.880
Influenced by a resurgent
indigenous view of Pachamama,

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.480
Bolivia and Ecuador have passed
laws granting all nature

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.080
equal rights with humans.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.240
And in New Zealand,
a Maori tribe has successfully fought

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.440
to have their river -
and ancestor -

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:15.000
given the same legal rights
as a person.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.400
In an interconnected world,

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:22.560
can we humans find ways to truly
reconnect to all life on the planet?

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:26.800
A new era might start
with one single idea.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.400
There is no Earth and us.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.960
We are one.

